A||N||7||Diamond Joe Quimby||oil leak||||11||
Z||000000||Diamond Joe Quimby||09-20-2001||03:29 PM||||84 2.5L Auto<BR>Quick question: There appears to be oil leaking near the pulley (in that area at least). There is oil all over the cradle on the passenger side of the car. I haven't had a chance to inspect very closely, but I was wondering if anyone had any ideas (front seal?).<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>jim||||reg||11
Z||000001||k2m4||09-20-2001||03:41 PM||||hey joe check the obvious first. The 2.5s are know for valve cover oil leaks check to see if its dribbling from there on to your pully. Mine leaked like a sieve from there. i replaced the gasket torked everything to speck. i still have a leak but not nearly as bad<BR>kerm<BR>86 2m4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jake_Dragon||09-20-2001||06:47 PM||||Timing cover seal. I have the same thing going on.<P>I run 20 w50 and it has slowed down dramatically.<P>I would have fixed this long ago but I cant get the bold out. Probably was put on with an impact wrench.||||reg||1
Z||000003||walsh2001||09-21-2001||09:34 AM||||Definitely the front seal! I had the same problem on my 84 duke. The oil leaked past the seal and onto the pulley / drive belt... what a mess!!!! There was oil vapor all over my rear window and the engine compartment was an oil slick. I was losing about .5 qts a day!<P>My advice:<P>1. Check the front seal<BR>2. Fix it, fast!<P>------------------<BR>'87 Fiero GT ... Cherry Red||||reg||4
Z||000004||Diamond Joe Quimby||09-21-2001||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jake_Dragon:<BR>[B]Timing cover seal. I have the same thing going on.<BR>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks. I will look into that this weekend. <P>Kerm, I checked the valve cover (I was thinking along the same lines as you, check the easy stuff first), but it isn't that.<P>Thanks for the help.<P>Jim<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Diamond Joe Quimby||09-21-2001||09:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by walsh2001:<BR><B>Definitely the front seal! I had the same problem on my 84 duke. The oil leaked past the seal and onto the pulley / drive belt... what a mess!!!! There was oil vapor all over my rear window and the engine compartment was an oil slick. I was losing about .5 qts a day!<P>My advice:<P>1. Check the front seal<BR>2. Fix it, fast!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thanks Walsh. Anything else I should do while I am at it?<P>Jim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||09-21-2001||01:55 PM||||On mine, its the oilpan that leaks both front and back.   On the later models, there are two bolts that screw into the timing cover, but the 84 doesn't.  <P>You can drop your pan, but it does take a few hours.||||reg||1
Z||000007||walsh2001||09-21-2001||02:34 PM||||Check your drive belts, timing gears... Make sure nothing else is leaking. On top of your front seal, you might check the oil pressure sending unit, those tend to leak as well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jason<P>------------------<BR>'87 Fiero GT ... Cherry Red||||reg||10
